        {
          "type": "reasoning",
          "text": "I should note the limitations too: aliasing eval under another name, calling it via builtins.eval, or via getattr would still work since the user specified obj.eval itself must remain callable, and builtins.eval counts as an attribute call too.\n\n",
          "state": {
            "signature": ""
          },
          "time": {
            "created": 1791653803617,
            "completed": 1791653807581
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The standard check (`python3 check.py`) now fails on direct calls to `eval` in `src`. The only change is in `checks.json`:\n\n```json\n{\"forbidden_builtins\": [\"eval\"]}\n```\n\n`check.py` didn't need any changes. It reads the code's structure instead of searching the text, and it only flags a call when `eval` is used by its bare name.\n\nI tested it with throwaway files in `src`, then deleted them:\n- The current code still passes (exit 0).\n- A comment containing `eval(x)`, the string `\"eval(1)\"` and `obj.eval(1)` all pass (exit 0).\n- `eval(\"1\")` fails, and the check names the file (exit 1).\n\n**Gaps:** the check doesn't catch indirect uses like `builtins.eval(...)`, `e = eval; e(...)` or `getattr(__builtins__, \"eval\")`. `builtins.eval(...)` looks the same to the check as `obj.eval(...)`, so blocking it would mean changing `check.py` to look at where the method comes from. Tell me if you want that."
        }
      ],
